.TH JITTERENTROPY-RNGD 1 2026-10-29 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "User Commands"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jitterentropy-rngd \- CPU Jitter Random Number Generator Daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jitterentropy-rngd
.RI [ option " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jitterentropy-rngd
collects entropy from CPU execution time jitter with the CPU Jitter Random
Number Generator of
.BR jitterentropy (3)
and hands it to the kernel's random number generator through
.IR /dev/random .
How the kernel takes it, and when it wants more, depends on the system.
.SS Linux
The daemon injects it through the
.B RNDADDENTROPY
ioctl of
.I /dev/random
described in
.BR random (4).
It must be started as root.
.PP
Every injection hands 64 bytes of Jitter RNG output to the kernel and claims
full entropy for them, followed by a request to reseed the kernel's random
number generator
.RB ( RNDRESEEDCRNG ).
The daemon injects:
.IP \(bu 3
once at startup. When the invocation returns, the kernel
has therefore been seeded, unless that injection failed, which the daemon
reports as a warning (see
.BR \-\-verbose ).
The kernel schedules during the injection, so it may still report
insufficient entropy right after the start;
.IP \(bu 3
whenever the kernel reports
.I /dev/random
as writable;
.IP \(bu 3
when
.I /proc/sys/kernel/random/entropy_avail
is below
.IR /proc/sys/kernel/random/write_wakeup_threshold ,
which it checks every 5 seconds during the first 10 minutes and every 50
seconds afterwards;
.IP \(bu 3
every 10 minutes regardless of the kernel's entropy estimate.
.PP
These are the defaults of
.B \-\-phase1
and
.BR \-\-phase2 ;
.B \-\-interval
replaces them with an alarm every so many seconds that injects each time.
.PP
On a kernel with the LRNG
.RI ( /proc/lrng_type
exists) only the first two apply and no reseed is requested: the LRNG reseeds
by itself. There is no schedule to set, and
.BR \-\-interval ,
.B \-\-phase1
and
.B \-\-phase2
end the daemon at its start.
.PP
After the first seed, and under
.B \-\-oneshot
before it, the daemon keeps only the capabilities
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
and, for the memory lock of a new instance,
.BR CAP_IPC_LOCK .
.B \-\-generate
keeps only the latter, once its file is open.
.PP
From its start, including under
.BR \-\-generate ,
it is not dumpable: it leaves no core dump and only a process with
.B CAP_SYS_PTRACE
can attach to it. It also sets
.B no_new_privs
and, from Linux 6.3 on, refuses memory that becomes executable.
.SS FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and macOS
None of these kernels has an entropy estimate to poll. The daemon writes 64
bytes of Jitter RNG output to
.I /dev/random
once at startup, before the invocation returns, and every 10 minutes after
.RB ( \-\-interval ).
FreeBSD feeds every write to its Fortuna generator, OpenBSD and macOS reseed
theirs with it at once. None counts it as entropy, except NetBSD for a write
by root, which counts at one bit per bit.
.PP
Writing to
.I /dev/random
takes root on FreeBSD and OpenBSD to open the node, and on NetBSD 10 and later
for every write: NetBSD checks the writer, not the opener, and refuses
anyone else with
.BR EPERM .
On macOS anyone may write. The daemon writes the first seed with the
privileges it was started with and then, when started as root, switches to
the user of
.B \-\-user
for good - which on NetBSD is none by default, so that it stays root. It keeps
.I /dev/random
open for writing, which needs no privilege once open. A seed the kernel
refuses to the user switched to ends the daemon, as no later one could
succeed.
.PP
After that, it confines itself to writing
.I /dev/random
and computing: with Capsicum
.RB ( capsicum (4))
on FreeBSD, with
.BR pledge (2)
on OpenBSD outside FIPS and NTG.1 mode. Under the
.BR pledge (2)
promises a new Jitter RNG instance cannot lock its memory, which FIPS and
NTG.1 mode and JENT_FORCE_SECURE_MEM demand, so these run unpledged. NetBSD and macOS have no such
sandbox.
.SS All systems
The schedule of the seeds and the first seed the kernel takes are logged
whatever
.B \-\-verbose
says, on Linux with the kernel it met: its
.BR uname (2),
whether it has the LRNG, the outcome of
.BR RNDRESEEDCRNG ,
its entropy settings and
.IR /proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled .
.PP
An injection that fails - on an intermittent health test failure the Jitter
RNG could not recover from, or on an injection the kernel refuses - makes the
daemon initialize the Jitter RNG anew and try again, up to five times, before
it waits for the next occasion. That a failure outlasted these retries is
logged whatever
.B \-\-verbose
says, once, as is the first injection to work again. A permanent health test
failure ends the daemon instead (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ):
a Jitter RNG that reported one must not be used any more, and a new instance
would only start its health tests afresh. The health tests report failures in
FIPS and NTG.1 mode only -
.BR \-\-fips ,
.BR \-\-ntg1 ,
or a system in FIPS mode.
.PP
The daemon detaches before it allocates the Jitter RNG, whose memory locks in
FIPS and NTG.1 mode a forked child would not inherit, and the invocation
returns after the first injection, with the exit status of a daemon that ended
before it. Started by init it does not fork, and writes the
.B \-\-pid
file all the same.
.PP
The Jitter RNG works in virtual machines as well as on bare metal, provided a
high-resolution time stamp is available. Where the library has no CPU counter
to read and uses
.BR clock_gettime (2),
the time stamp comes from the kernel's clock source; on Linux,
.I /sys/devices/system/clocksource/clocksource0/current_clocksource
names it, and it can be changed by writing one of the names in
.I available_clocksource
beside it.
.PP
With
.B \-\-generate
it writes bytes of the Jitter RNG to standard output or a file instead and
exits: nothing goes to the kernel, and neither root nor
.I /dev/random
is needed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log more. Without it only errors and notices are logged; given once warnings
as well,
twice the wakeups and injections and the status document of
.BR jent_status (3)
after each initialization of the Jitter RNG - at the start, by the daemon
after a failed injection, and by the Jitter RNG itself recovering from an
intermittent health test failure - three times debugging messages. A detached
daemon has its standard output redirected to
.IR /dev/null ,
so
.B \-\-syslog
is needed to see any of it.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-foreground
Stay in the foreground instead of detaching from the invoking terminal.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-syslog
Send the log messages to
.BR syslog (3)
with the
.B LOG_DAEMON
facility instead of writing them to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIfile\fR"
Write the PID to
.I file
and lock it, so that a second instance naming the same file refuses to start
and exits with 1.
An existing
.I file
must be a regular file of the daemon's user with no other link, and not a
symbolic link; anything else ends the start with 1, untouched.
The file is only written when the daemon detaches, not with
.BR \-\-foreground ,
and is removed when the daemon exits - except where it switched to another
user or confined itself, which leaves the file behind for the next start to
take over.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sp800\-90b
Read with
.BR jent_read_entropy ()
instead of
.BR jent_read_entropy_safe ()
of
.BR jent_read_entropy (3),
so that the oversampling rate is not raised after a health test failure: the
entropy rate assumed for the noise source stays the assessed one, as SP800-90B
requires. The option does not enable the FIPS mode of the Jitter RNG; that is
.BR \-\-fips .
.TP
.B \-\-fips
Allocate the Jitter RNG in FIPS mode, with the SP800-90B health tests
enforced: adds
.B JENT_FORCE_FIPS
to
.BR \-\-flags .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-ntg1
Allocate the Jitter RNG in AIS 20/31 NTG.1 mode: adds
.B JENT_NTG1
to
.BR \-\-flags .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-force\-internal\-timer
Use the internal timer of the Jitter RNG, a counting thread, instead of the
CPU's time stamp: adds
.B JENT_FORCE_INTERNAL_TIMER
to
.BR \-\-flags .
The Jitter RNG refuses it together with
.B \-\-ntg1
or
.BR \-\-disable\-internal\-timer .
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-disable\-internal\-timer
Never fall back to the internal timer: adds
.B JENT_DISABLE_INTERNAL_TIMER
to
.BR \-\-flags .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-flags " \fIflags\fR"
The flags the Jitter RNG is allocated with, as a decimal number: the sum of
the
.B JENT_*
values described in
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
such as 32 for
.B JENT_FORCE_FIPS
or 64 for
.BR JENT_NTG1 ;
.BR jitterentropy-flags (1)
computes and decodes it.
The default is 0. The options above add their flag to it in whichever order
they are given.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-osr " \fIosr\fR"
The oversampling rate the Jitter RNG is allocated with. The default is the
library's minimum of 3.
.TP
.BR \-\-phase1 " \fIperiod\fR:\fIwakeups\fR"
Linux only. Check the kernel's entropy estimate every
.I period
seconds, and after
.I wakeups
checks inject regardless of it and move on to phase 2. The default is 5:120.
.TP
.BR \-\-phase2 " \fIperiod\fR:\fIwakeups\fR"
Linux only. The same for everything after phase 1, injecting regardless of the
estimate after every
.I wakeups
checks. The default is 50:12. Neither value of either phase may be 0, and on a
kernel with the LRNG neither phase may be given.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-interval " \fIseconds\fR"
Seed the kernel every
.I seconds
after the first; 0 is refused. On FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and macOS the
default is 600. On Linux the alarm goes off every
.I seconds
in either phase and injects each time, regardless of the kernel's entropy
estimate, in place of the checks of
.B \-\-phase1
and
.BR \-\-phase2 ,
neither of which goes with it; without it, those apply. On a kernel with the
LRNG it is refused.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " \fIuser\fR"
FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and macOS only. The user to switch to after the
first seed when the daemon was started as root, together with that user's
group. An empty
.I user
keeps root. The default is
.IR nobody ,
and empty on NetBSD, whose kernel takes a write to
.I /dev/random
from root alone from NetBSD 10 on. The daemon exits with 1 when the user does
not exist, the switch fails, or the kernel refuses a seed to the user.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " \fIpath\fR"
FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and macOS only. Write to
.I path
instead of
.IR /dev/random ,
which must exist; a regular file collects the seeds for testing.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the status document of
.BR jent_status (3)
for a Jitter RNG allocated with the
.BR \-\-flags ,
.BR \-\-osr
and the options adding to
.B \-\-flags
given, to standard error, and exit without injecting anything. Give the
options the daemon runs with.
.TP
.B \-\-oneshot
Seed the kernel once in the foreground and exit, for example from an initramfs
or a service of
.IR Type=oneshot .
A failed injection is retried as described above; no PID file is written.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-generate " \fIbytes\fR"
Write
.I bytes
bytes of the Jitter RNG to standard output, or to the file of
.BR \-\-output ,
and exit without seeding the kernel; the log goes to standard error. The
Jitter RNG is allocated as for seeding, with
.BR \-\-flags ,
.BR \-\-osr ,
.B \-\-sp800\-90b
and the options adding to
.BR \-\-flags .
A failed read ends the run instead of initializing the Jitter RNG anew, and
what was written by then stays. 0 is refused, and so are
.B \-\-oneshot
and
.B \-\-status
with it, and a closed standard output without
.BR \-\-output .
.TP
.BR \-\-output " \fIfile\fR"
The file
.B \-\-generate
writes to: created, or truncated when it is a regular file of the user's own
with no other link, and set to mode 0600 either way. A symbolic link, a hard
link, a file of another user, a FIFO or a device is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-hex
Have
.B \-\-generate
write two lowercase hex digits per byte, with no newline at the end.
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-on\-error
Exit on any error instead of trying to recover, so that a supervising
monitor can act on it.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version of the daemon and of the Jitter RNG library and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after
.BR \-\-version ,
after
.B \-\-oneshot
seeded the kernel, after
.B \-\-generate
wrote all its bytes, and when the daemon was terminated by
.BR SIGTERM ,
.BR SIGINT ,
.B SIGQUIT
or
.BR SIGHUP ,
on which it shuts down cleanly. 1 for an error: on Linux no root privileges, a
Jitter RNG that cannot be initialized or allocated, a file that cannot be
opened, a user that cannot be switched to or whose seeds the kernel refuses, a
sandbox that cannot be entered, a schedule given on a kernel with the LRNG,
an invalid option, a
.B \-\-oneshot
whose injections all failed, a
.B \-\-generate
whose read or write failed, and also after
.BR \-\-help .
.B \-\-status
exits with a non-zero status after printing the document.
.PP
A permanent health test failure of the Jitter RNG ends the daemon with 2,
with
.B \-\-exit\-on\-error
or without, under
.B \-\-oneshot
and
.B \-\-generate
as well.
.PP
With
.B \-\-exit\-on\-error
a failed injection ends the daemon with a status for its
.IR errno ,
be it of the read, of the failing ioctl or
.BR write (2),
or of
.BR pselect (2)
waiting for the next occasion: 3 for
.BR EIO ,
which a health test failure the Jitter RNG did not recover from gives, 4 for
.BR EINVAL ,
which a read without an instance or a timer gives, 5 for
.B EPERM
or
.BR EACCES ,
and 1 for any other. Every other failure - opening a device, the PID file, the
startup of the Jitter RNG - exits with 1, with the option or without.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ", " SIGQUIT ", " SIGHUP
Shut the daemon down cleanly, with exit status 0.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Log the status document of
.BR jent_status (3)
for the Jitter RNG in use, whatever
.B \-\-verbose
says, at the
.I notice
level of
.BR syslog (3)
with
.BR \-\-syslog .
One sent while the daemon starts up waits until it runs, rather than ending
it. On Linux the daemon reads the signal from a
.BR signalfd (2);
on FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and macOS it keeps it blocked except while it
waits for the next seed, which the signal does not put off.
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
Seed the kernel now. On Linux: inject 64 bytes of the Jitter RNG and
request a reseed of the kernel's random number generator
.RB ( RNDRESEEDCRNG ),
as the alarm forces one. On a kernel with the LRNG the injection alone: the
LRNG reseeds by itself, and no reseed is requested of it. On FreeBSD, NetBSD,
OpenBSD and macOS: write 64 bytes to
.IR /dev/random ,
as each
.B \-\-interval
does. The schedule stays as it was, the outcome is logged at the
.I notice
level, and the signal is handled as
.BR SIGUSR1 .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Linux only. Where set, as under a systemd service of
.IR Type=notify ,
the daemon sends
.I READY=1
to it once the kernel accepted an injection for the first time, as
.BR sd_notify (3)
describes.
.TP
.BR NO_COLOR ", " TERM
The log on standard output - on standard error with
.B \-\-generate
- is colored when that is a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is unset or
.IR dumb .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/random
The device the entropy is injected into.
.TP
.IR /proc/sys/kernel/random/entropy_avail ", " /proc/sys/kernel/random/write_wakeup_threshold
Linux: the kernel's entropy estimate and the threshold below which the daemon
injects.
.TP
.I /proc/lrng_type
Linux: its existence marks a kernel with the LRNG.
.TP
.I jitterentropy.service
Linux: the systemd unit installed with the daemon. It starts the daemon in the
foreground before
.IR sysinit.target ,
and is active once the daemon has seeded the kernel for the first time.
.TP
.I jitterentropy_rngd
FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD: the
.BR rc.d (8)
script installed with the daemon, enabled with
.I jitterentropy_rngd_enable=YES
in
.BR rc.conf (5)
on FreeBSD,
.I jitterentropy_rngd=YES
on NetBSD and
.B rcctl enable jitterentropy_rngd
on OpenBSD.
.TP
.I jitterentropy-rngd.plist
macOS: the
.BR launchd.plist (5)
installed with the daemon, which runs it as
.I nobody
from boot.
.SH NOTES
Without
.B \-\-pid
and
.B \-\-output
the daemon writes to no file system object and runs on a file system mounted
read-only.
.PP
Standard input, output and error that are closed when the daemon starts are
opened on
.IR /dev/null ,
so that neither
.I /dev/random
nor the
.BR syslog (3)
socket takes their numbers.
.PP
The output of the Jitter RNG is considered to hold full entropy, which is why
the daemon claims it for every byte it injects where the kernel takes a claim:
on Linux, and for the writes by root on NetBSD.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clock_gettime (2),
.BR pledge (2),
.BR jent_entropy_collector_alloc (3),
.BR jent_read_entropy (3),
.BR jent_status (3),
.BR jitterentropy (3),
.BR jitterentropy-flags (1),
.BR capsicum (4),
.BR random (4),
.BR launchd.plist (5),
.BR rc.d (8)
